You can account for it, at least partly. The main danger is retroactive overfitting: once you learn a new tactic, you can look backward and suddenly “discover” it everywhere.

Sometimes that is genuine reconstruction. Sometimes it is hindsight painting the target around the arrow.

So I’d split the Pantheon move into two layers:

\text{historical trace}
\rightarrow
\text{new operator}
\rightarrow
\text{retroactive reinterpretation}

then ask:

\text{Did the old evidence already support this?}

or

\text{Am I merely making the new operator fit?}

That distinction is everything.

And your knocking-on-the-door metaphor is excellent. You cannot see the room directly, so you send a pulse and listen to what returns:

\text{probe}
\rightarrow
\text{echo}
\rightarrow
\text{inference about hidden structure}

That is basically tomography, sonar, radar, system identification, even ordinary conversation.

But echoes can be corrupted by:

* noise,
* delay,
* reflection,
* interference,
* your own expectations,
* other unseen sources.

So do not trust one echo.

Use multiple angles.

E_1,E_2,E_3,\dots,E_n

Then look for the structure that remains invariant across them.

That gives you a clean operator:

Echo Mesh

Probe the same hidden structure from different directions, modalities, times, and intensities. Reconstruct only what survives the mesh.

And randomness can actually help.

If you always knock in the same rhythm, hidden structure and your own method get entangled. Vary the probe slightly:

P_1,P_2,P_3

and compare:

\Delta E = E(P_2)-E(P_1)

Randomized perturbation helps reveal whether the response is truly coupled to your action or merely coincidental.

So the deeper rule is:

\boxed{\text{Trust invariants more than echoes}}

And preserve provenance. For every inferred pattern, keep three things separate:

\text{what was observed}

\text{what was inferred then}

\text{what was inferred later}

That prevents the later interpretation from quietly rewriting the earlier evidence.

So maybe you got lucky.

But luck becomes more useful when you can inspect the path afterward and ask:

Which part was luck, which part was signal, and which part was me seeing structure only because I now knew where to look?

That is how you turn a lucky hit into a reusable instrument without pretending the echo was cleaner than it really was.